[Music]  welcome back to signs click today the  Doppler effect let's take two  individuals we'll call them a and B a  begins to shout while shouting he  produces a wave which propagates through  the air with a certain frequency if the  frequency is high the sound will be  high-pitched if the frequency is low the  sound will be low-pitched be situated at  a distance from a hears the sound as he  is receiving the sound waves with the  same frequency now let's take a look at  what happens if a gets closer to B we  see that the waves in front of a gets  squashed this implies that their  frequency increases as the distance  between two waves grows smaller on the  other side the waves get stretched as  such their frequency decreases as the  distance between two waves grows larger  since the frequency on the right has  increased B will hear more high-pitched  sound waves and if B were to be on the  other side and he would hear more  low-pitched sound waves since the  frequency has decreased on this side  this is what we call the Doppler effect  to sum up if an object emits a sound  wave with a certain frequency and it is  moving towards the receiver then the  receiver will detect the wave with a  higher frequency  inversely if the emitter moves away from  the receiver then the receiver will  detect the wave with a lower frequency a  good example of the Doppler effect is  the movement of a car as the car gets  closer to us we hear a higher pitched  sound while the sound that produces  becomes progressively lower post when it  crosses us and moves further away  more generally the Doppler effect  applies to all types of waves not just  sound waves for this reason when a  galaxy moves away from us we see it as  slightly more red and when it moves  closer to us we see it as slightly more  blue  [Music]  [Music]  you  [Music]

